# CASP15 T1187 — The Sugar Reader (8AD2)
---

Plants can't run from danger.
Instead, they have sophisticated defense systems
that detect threats at the molecular level.

This is Nictaba — a lectin from tobacco plants.

Published in Glycobiology in 2024 by Bloch and colleagues.

> close
> load 8ad2
---

Nictaba is a sugar-binding protein — a lectin —
that reads the sugar coatings on molecules
and cells.

It forms a dimer, and this crystal structure
revealed a completely new mode of lectin
dimerization that hadn't been seen before.

> spin 0.15
---

The carbohydrate binding site is where
Nictaba recognizes specific sugar patterns.

Lectins like this are part of the plant's
innate immune system — they detect
foreign sugar signatures on pathogens
and trigger defense responses.

> stop
> view ligand
---

Lectins are incredibly useful in biotechnology.
They're used in blood typing, cancer research,
and as tools to study the complex sugar
coatings on cells.

Understanding new ways that lectins bind
sugars opens doors for designing better
molecular recognition tools.

> reset
